          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the symptoms and abnormal findings chapter for, and when should a code from it be used instead of a disease code?",
          "answer": "The symptoms and abnormal findings chapter (ICD-10-CM Chapter 18, codes R00 to R99) is for recording a symptom, sign or abnormal result when no more specific diagnosis has been established. Its codes start with the letter R. (Source: coding-icd10-chapter-18-symptoms.)\n\nA symptom code is used when the record shows a symptom, sign or abnormal result and no more specific diagnosis has been made. Once a diagnosis is documented, the diagnosis code replaces the symptom code. The one exception is a symptom that is not routinely part of that diagnosis. That symptom can be reported alongside the diagnosis because it adds information the diagnosis does not. (Source: coding-icd10-chapter-18-symptoms.)\n\nSome examples from the retrieved guidance:\n- A single raised blood pressure reading, or several, in someone without a hypertension diagnosis is a finding and is classified in Chapter 18. The diagnosis in the record, not the number itself, decides whether the hypertension code applies. (Source: coding-hypertension-i10.)\n- Fluid build-up, leg swelling and lung congestion documented without a heart failure diagnosis stay in Chapter 18. (Source: coding-heart-failure-i50.)\n- Heartburn or regurgitation with no reflux disease documented is coded as a symptom. Where reflux disease is documented, those symptoms are part of it and are not coded separately. (Source: coding-gerd-k21.)\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover the full list of R codes or the detailed rules for every symptom, so I cannot say more than this from them. Coding decisions are made by trained coders working from the full official manual and the clinical record.\n\nIf you are asking because of a symptom you or someone else has, a clinician is the right person to talk to about what it means. If symptoms are severe, sudden or getting worse, seek urgent medical care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
